<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emily Ruete</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1844–1924</span> · Also known as Sayyida Salme, Salme bint Said</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emily Ruete, born Sayyida Salme, was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-sultan">Said bin Sultan</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and Zanzibar. In 1866 she eloped with the German merchant Rudolph Heinrich Ruete, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. Her Memoirs of an Arabian Princess, published in 1886, describe life in the sultan's palaces and are a rare insider account of the Zanzibar court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emily-ruete.jpg" alt="Emily Ruete" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Plate, Heinrich Friedrich (* 1824, † 1895), Fotograf in Hamburg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emily_Ruete_(Sayyida_Salme),_Princess_of_Zanzibar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
